<BaseLayout title="Views from Up High — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Cities seen from above, 2022–2026: three Ferris wheels, four high bars and rooftops, seven observation decks from Baltimore to the 116th floor in Shenzhen, five hills above cities, from Griffith Observatory to the Baldwin Hills in wildfire smoke, and five hotel windows from Times Square to Waikīkī.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>Views from Up High</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Wheels, rooftops, towers and hills: every city I have looked down on, roughly from the lowest to the highest.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>26 vantage points · 16 cities · March 2022 – September 2026</strong><br />
    3 wheels · 4 high bars and rooftops · 7 observation decks · 5 hills above cities · 5 hotel windows · 1 balloon · 1 plane window<br />
    Lowest: a converted post office's roof in Houston · highest: the 116th floor of the Ping An Finance Centre,
    more than half a kilometre above Shenzhen · most visited: the High Roller, Twin Peaks and Griffith Observatory, three times each
  </div>

  <p>
    Going through four years of photos for the other posts on this page, I kept running into the same kind of picture.
    It is taken through glass, or over a railing, or from the top of a hill. A city is spread out below, with its
    streets reduced to lines and its tallest buildings reduced to something you could pick up. Wherever we
    go, it seems, we end up going up something: a Ferris wheel, a high bar, a tower with an elevator, a hill with a parking lot.
    This post collects them in one place.
  </p>
  <p>
    The order is roughly by height, starting with the wheels and ending with the hills, which are a different kind of
    high. Most of these views already appear in a trip post, and each section links to it. A few, like Seattle, Vancouver
    and most of the Los Angeles ones, appear here for the first time. Heights are the published figures for the
    buildings and hills, not anything I measured.
  </p>

  <h2>Wheels</h2>

  <h3>SkyStar, Golden Gate Park · May 2022</h3>
  <p>
    The lowest wheel in the set, at about 150 feet, and the gentlest. SkyStar was put up at the Music Concourse in
    2020 for Golden Gate Park's 150th anniversary and was meant to be temporary. We rode it after lunch on May 20th,
    2022. It is not high enough to show you the city, but it is just high enough to show you the park: the living roof of
    the California Academy of Sciences, two and a half acres of native plants on green humps, and Sutro Tower standing
    on its hill behind. The full day is in <a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">Road Trips, 2022</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'skystar-2022.jpg'} alt="The green living roof of the California Academy of Sciences seen from above, with Sutro Tower on the hill behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Academy's living roof and Sutro Tower from SkyStar, May 20, 2022.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Centennial Wheel, Navy Pier, Chicago · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    About two hundred feet up, at the end of a long road trip. The Centennial Wheel opened in 2016 for the hundredth
    birthday of Navy Pier, and the Ferris wheel itself was invented for Chicago's 1893 World's Fair, so riding one
    here felt like the right thing to do. From the top on August 10th, 2024, the carousel and the pier's long roofs were
    straight below, and the whole skyline fit in one frame, with Lake Point Tower standing alone on the lake side of
    Lake Shore Drive. More in <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">the 2024 road trip</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'centennial-2024.jpg'} alt="View down onto Navy Pier, its blue-roofed carousel and the Chicago skyline from the top of the Centennial Wheel." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Navy Pier and the skyline, August 10, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The High Roller, Las Vegas · 2022, 2023, 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The High Roller is 550 feet tall and opened behind the LINQ in 2014. A full turn takes about half an hour,
    slow enough that you stop noticing you are moving. I have ridden it three times. The first was on March 16th,
    2022, at the end of a desert road trip, with a squall over the mountains. The third was on June 10th, 2024, with
    my parents. Both times, at the top, I took nearly the same photo: the Flamingo's white towers and pool garden
    below, and the central Strip behind.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-2022.jpg'} alt="The Flamingo hotel and the towers of the central Strip from the High Roller, with mountains and clouds beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Flamingo, March 16, 2022, 4:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-2024.jpg'} alt="View from the High Roller over the Flamingo's pool garden and white hotel towers, with more resort towers beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Flamingo again, June 10, 2024, 1:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The ride in between was at night, on September 3rd, 2023, the weekend we went to see Adele. At night the wheel is a
    different thing. The Venetian's long gold façade was lit up like a stage set, Harrah's red sign glowed below it,
    and the grid of the city ran out in tiny lights to the dark mountains. The posts for those trips are
    <a href="/misc/desert-2022">Death Valley, Vegas &amp; Page</a>, <a href="/misc/labor-day-2023">Rainbows, Adele
    and the Sphere</a> and <a href="/misc/las-vegas">Las Vegas, Again</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'high-roller-2023.jpg'} alt="Night view from the High Roller over the illuminated Venetian and neighboring towers, with the city lights stretching to the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking north at night, September 3, 2023.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Rooftops and high bars</h2>

  <h3>POST Houston · July 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The lowest view in this post, and still a good one. POST Houston was the city's main post office, built in 1962,
    and in 2021 it reopened as a food hall and concert venue with a huge rooftop park and farm on top. We went up on the
    night of July 3rd, 2023, the night before the Fourth. Across the freeway, the downtown towers were lit white, gold and
    purple. It was a hot, still Houston night, and the rooftop was dotted with people at tables in the dark. The rest of
    that trip is in <a href="/misc/texas-2023">Texas, 2023</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'post-houston.jpg'} alt="The downtown Houston skyline at night from the POST Houston roof, with towers lit in white, gold and purple." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Houston from the roof, July 3, 2023.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>A sky lounge in Sanya · February 2023</h3>
  <p>
    On the afternoon of February 8th, 2023, we had afternoon tea in a bar high up a building above Sanya Bay, on the
    southern tip of Hainan. The building's façade has angled metal fins, so the view comes in slices. Between two of
    them the water ran straight across to the city and the green hills behind it. Turning the other way, the egg-shaped
    towers of Phoenix Island were close enough to count the balconies. The trip is in
    <a href="/misc/china-2023">China, 2023</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sanya-lounge.jpg'} alt="A view of Sanya Bay and the city framed between two angled metal fins of a building facade." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sanya Bay, between the fins, February 8, 2023.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>71Above, U.S. Bank Tower, Los Angeles · April 2022</h3>
  <p>
    The U.S. Bank Tower is 1,018 feet tall. When it was finished in 1989 it was the tallest building in Los Angeles, and
    for almost thirty years it stayed that way. 71Above is the restaurant on its 71st floor. We had dinner there on
    April 9th, 2022, timed for the sunset. At 7:17 pm the sun went down into a haze over the ocean side of the basin.
    Downtown's towers stood in silhouette below the windows, and the Wilshire Grand's spire, the building that took the
    title in 2017, rose against the orange sky.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '71above.jpg'} alt="Sunset over Los Angeles through tall restaurant windows on the 71st floor, with downtown towers below and the spire of the Wilshire Grand against an orange sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>From the 71st floor, April 9, 2022, 7:17 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Spire 73, Wilshire Grand Center · December 2022</h3>
  <p>
    Eight months later we were on the other side of that view, at Spire 73, the bar at the top of the Wilshire Grand.
    The tower is 1,100 feet to the tip of its spire, and its top floors hold a hotel lobby, restaurants and the bar. We
    were there with a friend on the evening of December 11th, 2022. Most of the photos from that
    evening are of the friend, which is as it should be. The one view I kept is a corner of the window at 8:24 pm,
    looking straight down on the lit sign of One Wilshire and the streets running off into the dark.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'spire-73.jpg'} alt="Looking down through glass at night on a dark office tower with ONE WILSHIRE lit on its top, and city lights and a lit freeway beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>One Wilshire, straight down, December 11, 2022, 8:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Observation decks</h2>

  <h3>Top of the World, Baltimore · July 2026</h3>
  <p>
    Baltimore's World Trade Center is 405 feet tall. It was finished in 1977 to a design by I. M. Pei's firm and is a
    regular pentagon in plan, which makes it the tallest pentagonal building in the world. It stands on a pier at the
    edge of the Inner Harbor. The Top of the World is the glass-walled observation level on the 27th floor. On
    July 5th, 2026, a little after one in the afternoon, the view south showed the harbor's basin, the marinas, the
    brick promenade and Federal Hill rising behind them. The day is in <a href="/misc/july4-2026">the July 4th post</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baltimore-wtc.jpg'} alt="The Inner Harbor basin with marinas, the brick promenade and Federal Hill, seen from high above." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The basin and Federal Hill, July 5, 2026.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Vancouver Lookout · September 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The Vancouver Lookout sits on top of Harbour Centre, which opened in 1977. You ride up in a glass elevator on the
    outside of the building and come out into a round room with windows all the way around. We went up on
    September 30th, 2023, a clear autumn day. Downtown was a crowd of towers, old stone and new glass, right below us.
    To the east the city went flat and green all the way to the towers of Metrotown in Burnaby, and a rooftop of trees
    just turning orange sat on a new building in the foreground. There is no post about this trip yet, so these two
    photos are new here.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vancouver-downtown.jpg'} alt="Downtown Vancouver from above: glass and stone towers, rooftops and streets in bright sun, with the water beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown from the Lookout, September 30, 2023, 1:08 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'vancouver-east.jpg'} alt="A new tower with autumn trees on its roof in the foreground, and the flat green east side of Vancouver stretching to a distant cluster of towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>East to Metrotown, 1:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>The STRAT, Las Vegas · 2022 and 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The STRAT is a 1,149-foot tower at the north end of the Strip. It opened in 1996 as the Stratosphere, and its top
    holds thrill rides, a revolving restaurant and two observation decks. I have been up twice. The first time was at
    9:09 pm on March 15th, 2022, when the whole valley was a sheet of lights running out to the mountains. The second
    was at 2:54 pm on April 8th, 2023. By daylight the north end of the Strip turned out to be mostly empty lots and
    parking, with a few towers in blue glass and red, and the mountains on the horizon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strat-2022.jpg'} alt="Las Vegas at night from the top of the STRAT: a grid of lights stretching to the horizon beyond a railing." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>March 15, 2022, 9:09 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'strat-2023.jpg'} alt="Daytime aerial view from the STRAT over the north end of the Strip: towers in blue glass and red, empty lots and parking, mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>April 8, 2023, 2:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Sky View Observatory, Columbia Center, Seattle · June 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Columbia Center has been Seattle's tallest building since it was finished in 1985, and its Sky View Observatory is
    on the 73rd floor, about 900 feet up. That is a good deal higher than the top of the Space Needle. We went up on the
    evening of June 17th, 2023, under a flat grey sky. Just after seven the cloud broke over Puget Sound, and a band of
    light came down on the water beyond the waterfront and the Great Wheel. A few minutes earlier, looking south, the
    football stadium was packed for an event, with the baseball park beside it and the rail yards running past. These
    photos haven't been in a post before.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'seattle-sound.jpg'} alt="Downtown Seattle from high above: towers in the foreground, the waterfront and a small Ferris wheel below, and evening light breaking through clouds over Puget Sound." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Light over Puget Sound, June 17, 2023, 7:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'seattle-stadium.jpg'} alt="A football stadium packed with people seen from high above, with rail lines, a clock tower and warehouse districts around it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A full stadium, 7:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>360 Chicago · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The observation deck in the old John Hancock Center is on the 94th floor, about a thousand feet up. The building,
    finished in 1969, is the black tapering tower with X-braces on the outside, which make the whole façade into one stiff
    tube. We watched dusk fall from there on August 8th, 2024. The lakeshore curved north in one clean arc, with Lake
    Shore Drive tracing it and the beaches below. Behind us the grid of the city switched on, one light at a time. The
    full evening is in <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">the 2024 road trip</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'chicago-360.jpg'} alt="Chicago's lakefront and Lake Shore Drive at dusk from an observation deck, with reflections of the deck in the glass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lakefront at dusk, August 8, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>One World Observatory, New York · January 2023</h3>
  <p>
    One World Trade Center is 1,776 feet tall, and its observatory, which opened in 2015, takes up floors 100 to 102.
    We went up on New Year's Day, 2023, in the early afternoon. To the south, the afternoon sun was on the harbor and the
    water shone silver. The Statue of Liberty was a small dark shape on her island, with ferry wakes drawing white lines
    around her. The whole holiday is in <a href="/misc/newyear-2023">New Year's on the East Coast</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'one-world.jpg'} alt="Upper New York Bay from One World Observatory, the water shining silver in the afternoon sun, the Statue of Liberty in silhouette on her island." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The harbor and the Statue of Liberty, January 1, 2023.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Ping An Finance Centre, Shenzhen · February 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The top of the list. The Ping An Finance Centre is 599 metres tall. It was designed by Kohn Pedersen Fox and
    finished in 2017, and it is the second-tallest building in China. Its observation deck is on the 116th floor, more
    than half a kilometre above the street. We went up on the afternoon of February 11th, 2023. The windows lean out
    between heavy steel mullions, so you look down past your own feet onto the Civic Center's blue roof, shaped like a
    bird spreading its wings. Around three o'clock, cloud slid down off the northern mountains over the districts beyond the
    towers, and for a few minutes the city simply stopped at a white edge. More in <a href="/misc/china-2023">China, 2023</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ping-an-civic.jpg'} alt="View from the Ping An observation deck between two window mullions, down onto the Civic Center's blue and yellow roof and the parks and towers of Futian." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Civic Center from the 116th floor, February 11, 2023.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'ping-an-clouds.jpg'} alt="A tall skyscraper and its neighbors far below, with green parks and a low bank of cloud over the northern districts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cloud over the northern districts, 3:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Hills above cities</h2>
  <p>
    Hills are a different kind of high. There is no glass, and you are looking across at the city as much as down on
    it. You also share the view with whoever else drove up the hill that evening.
  </p>

  <h3>Mount Washington, Pittsburgh · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    Grandview Avenue runs along the edge of the bluff across the Monongahela from downtown, with overlooks built out over
    the drop. The Monongahela Incline has been carrying people up this slope since 1870. It was built for the
    steelworkers who lived on top of the hill and worked in the mills by the river. On August 6th, 2024, fast clouds
    were throwing shadows across downtown, the Point and the bridges, so the city kept flickering between light and shade.
    From <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">the 2024 road trip</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pittsburgh.jpg'} alt="Downtown Pittsburgh skyline and rivers from a hillside overlook, under big white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Pittsburgh from Grandview Avenue, August 6, 2024.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Baldwin Hills Scenic Overlook, Los Angeles · January 2025</h3>
  <p>
    This one is a piece of history I would rather not have photographed. On January 10th, 2025, three days after the Palisades and Eaton fires
    broke out, we went up to the Baldwin Hills Scenic Overlook in Culver City in the late afternoon. At 4:10 pm the
    downtown towers stood in a brown band of smoke that covered the whole sky above them. Twenty minutes later, a long
    plume lit gold by the low sun was drifting over the mountains on the far side of the basin. The neighborhoods below
    looked completely ordinary.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baldwin-skyline.jpg'} alt="Downtown Los Angeles in the distance under a thick brown band of smoke, with the flat city in golden light below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown under smoke, January 10, 2025, 4:10 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'baldwin-smoke.jpg'} alt="A long plume of wildfire smoke lit gold by the setting sun above distant mountains, with the city and dry grass in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The plume over the mountains, 4:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Twin Peaks, San Francisco · 2022, 2023, 2026</h3>
  <p>
    Twin Peaks is the pair of hills near the middle of San Francisco, a little over 900 feet high. I have been up three
    times, and the view changes completely with the light. At 6:25 pm on May 19th, 2022, the whole city was
    spread out in clear evening light, with the bay and the East Bay hills beyond. At 9:39 pm on
    November 24th, 2023, after Thanksgiving, it was dark, and Market Street ran down from the foot of the hill to the bay
    as a straight ribbon of orange lights.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-2022.jpg'} alt="Panorama of downtown San Francisco and the bay from the Twin Peaks overlook in early evening." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>May 19, 2022, 6:25 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-2023.jpg'} alt="Night view over San Francisco from Twin Peaks, with Market Street as a bright orange line of lights running toward the downtown towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>November 24, 2023, 9:39 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The third time was a Monday afternoon, May 25th, 2026, Memorial Day, under big clouds. Salesforce Tower, finished in 2018 and the tallest
    building in the city, stood over everything, with the Transamerica Pyramid to its left. The first two visits are in
    <a href="/misc/roadtrips-2022">Road Trips, 2022</a> and <a href="/misc/bay-area">The Bay Area, Again and Again</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'twin-peaks-2026.jpg'} alt="Downtown San Francisco from Twin Peaks by day under heavy clouds, with Salesforce Tower and the Transamerica Pyramid, and Market Street running toward them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>May 25, 2026, 3:44 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The West Hills, Portland · 2023 and 2025</h3>
  <p>
    Twice, two and a half years apart, we ended up at the same bend of road in the West Hills, just below Pittock
    Mansion. The view is the reason people go there. Downtown Portland is right below the trees, and Mt. Hood, 11,249
    feet high and about fifty miles to the east, stands alone on the horizon. At 4:20 pm on July 22nd, 2023, the
    mountain was a white cone in a hard blue summer sky. At 4:51 pm on November 30th, 2025, it was dusk, the downtown
    lights were coming on, and the snow on Hood had turned faintly pink. The winter visit is in
    <a href="/misc/oregon-2025">Oregon, 2025</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-2023.jpg'} alt="Downtown Portland on a clear summer day from the forested West Hills, with the snowy cone of Mt. Hood on the horizon under a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>July 22, 2023, 4:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'portland-2025.jpg'} alt="Downtown Portland at dusk seen from the forested West Hills, with the snow-covered cone of Mt. Hood glowing faintly pink on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>November 30, 2025, 4:51 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Griffith Observatory, Los Angeles · 2022, 2022, 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The highest of the hills, and the one closest to home. Griffith Observatory opened in 1935 on the south slope of
    Mount Hollywood, about 1,100 feet up, and its lawn is where Los Angeles goes to look at itself. I have photos from
    there on three evenings. The first was a night in March 2022, at 9:56 pm, when downtown was a small island of
    towers in a sea of lights. The second was on August 6th, 2022, at sunset, looking west toward the ocean.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'griffith-2022.jpg'} alt="Downtown Los Angeles at night from Griffith Observatory, a small cluster of lit towers in a wide plain of city lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>March 18, 2022, 9:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'griffith-2022-aug.jpg'} alt="An orange sunset band over the far edge of the Los Angeles basin, with city lights coming on below the dark hills." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>West at sunset, August 6, 2022, 8:18 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The third was on January 28th, 2024, a clear winter evening. At 5:20 pm the sky behind downtown went pink and
    orange, and the towers stood up sharp against it. Of all the views in this post, it is the one we can go back to
    on any evening we like.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'griffith-2024.jpg'} alt="The downtown Los Angeles skyline under a pink and orange sunset sky, seen across the city from Griffith Observatory." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown at sunset, January 28, 2024, 5:20 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Hotel windows</h2>
  <p>
    Some of the best views needed no ticket at all. In a city I always ask for the highest floor I can get, and
    these are the hotel rooms where it paid off. They all appear, with the hotels themselves, in
    <a href="/misc/hotels">Rooms with a View</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Hotel Edison, New York · New Year's Eve 2022</h3>
  <p>
    Our window looked straight down on Times Square. On New Year's Eve, at 8:42 pm, the billboards were blazing and
    the stage for the midnight countdown was already set up below.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-edison-times-square.jpg'} alt="Looking straight down from a hotel window onto the billboards and stage area of Times Square at night." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Times Square from the room, December 31, 2022.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Hyatt Regency Dallas · July 2023</h3>
  <p>
    Dallas from a room next to Reunion Tower, at night, with one of the downtown towers outlined in green.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-dallas-night.jpg'} alt="Dallas skyline at night with a tower outlined in green lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown Dallas, July 2, 2023, 9:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Sable at Navy Pier, Chicago · August 2024</h3>
  <p>
    The same skyline as the Centennial Wheel and 360 Chicago, but from the water side: the hotel sits on Navy Pier,
    so the window looks back across the harbor at the whole city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-sable-skyline.jpg'} alt="The Chicago skyline across the water from Navy Pier, with a cruise boat below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Chicago from Navy Pier, August 10, 2024, 9:07 am.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Hilton Sydney, 43rd floor · December 2023</h3>
  <p>
    The highest hotel room we know we have stayed in, right in the middle of the city. At sunset the towers around us lit up one
    by one.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-sydney-sunset.jpg'} alt="Sunset over the lit towers of central Sydney from a 43rd-floor window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sydney at sunset, December 14, 2023, 8:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Ka Laʻi Waikiki Beach · September 2026</h3>
  <p>
    And the gentlest view on this page: a balcony over the lawns of Fort DeRussy, with the sun going down into the
    Pacific beside the towers of Waikīkī.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hotel-kalai-sunset.jpg'} alt="Sunset over the ocean from a glass-railed balcony, with Waikīkī hotel towers along the shore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Waikīkī at sunset, September 11, 2026, 6:24 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Coda: above everything</h2>
  <p>
    Two more, from the only vantage points here that weren't standing on anything. On Easter Sunday, April 20th,
    2025, we rode the tethered Balloon Safari over the San Diego Zoo Safari Park. Looking straight down through the
    gondola's netting, the round shadow of our own balloon sat in the middle of the park's paths and enclosures. It is
    a picture of us without anyone in it. That day is in <a href="/misc/san-diego">San Diego</a>.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'balloon-shadow.jpg'} alt="Aerial view straight down through gondola netting onto park paths and enclosures, with the round shadow of the balloon in the middle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The balloon's shadow, April 20, 2025.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    And the highest of all, by a long way: a plane window on December 29th, 2022, at 7:11 pm, coming down over
    northern New Jersey toward Newark at the start of that New Year's trip. The wing light flared in the dark, and
    below it the suburbs were a carpet of orange streetlights all the way to the horizon. It is the only city in this
    post I couldn't name from the photo. The location data says Hasbrouck Heights.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'plane-newark.jpg'} alt="Night view from a plane window: a bright wing light in the dark, above a vast carpet of orange city lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Northern New Jersey on approach, December 29, 2022, 7:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Looking down</h2>
  <p>
    Putting these side by side, what strikes me is how much alike the cities look from above, and how different they
    are once you can name things. From high enough up, every downtown is a cluster of towers, a grid and some water.
    What makes it Seattle or Shenzhen or Pittsburgh is a stadium, a blue roof shaped like a bird, an old incline on
    a hill. From up there, half the fun is finding the places you walked past that morning.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other thing is that height isn't what makes a view. The 116th floor in Shenzhen is the record, and its photos are
    all taken through angled glass and reflections. Some of the best ones here were taken from a hill with a parking lot, or from a wheel
    that turns once every half hour.
  </p>
